, \"little\"]` is wrong. \"Mary\" has 3 consonants. Let me fix:\n\n- n=4 \u2192 [\"little\"]\n- n=3 \u2192 [\"Mary\", \"lamb\"]\n- n=2 \u2192 [\"had\"]\n\n\"simple\" \u2014 s, i, m, p, l, e: consonants s, m, p, l = 4. \"little\" = 4. \"sentence\" \u2014 s, e, n, t, e, n, c, e: consonants s, n, t, n, c = 5. So `select_words(\"simple little sentence\", 4) == [\"simple\", \"little\"]`.\n\nAlso test with punctuation maybe: \"hello world!\" \u2014 \"world!\" has consonants w, r, l, d, ! = 5. Not necessary but could add.\n\nAlso test that non-letter characters count as consonants (since the check is just \"not in vowels\"). E.g., select_words(\"a1b2\", 2) \u2014 \"a1b2\": a (vowel), 1 (consonant), b (consonant), 2 (consonant) = 3 consonants. Hmm.\n\nLet me also make sure the empty-string test covers the branch where the for loop at line 4 doesn't execute its body. And a test where a word has zero length? `s.split()` never yields empty strings, so len(word) >= 1 always. The inner loop `range(0, len(word))` with len >= 1 always executes at least once. So the branch where the inner loop body doesn't execute can't happen via split. But the branch at line 7 (false case, i.e., vowel) is covered by vowel-containing words.\n\nBranch coverage:\n- Line 4 loop: executes with words (normal test) and doesn't (empty string test). \u2713\n- Line 6 loop: always executes at least once per word since split() gives non-empty words. \u2713\n- Line 7: true (consonant \u2192 line 8) and false (vowel \u2192 skip line 8). \u2713\n- Line 9: true (append \u2192 line 10) and false (skip). \u2713\n\nCoverage of all lines 3-11: any normal test covers all lines. The empty string test covers line 3, 4, 11 but not 5-10. The vowel test covers line 7 false branch. The matching test covers lines 8 and 10.\n\nNow, about cleanup/state pollution: this function is pure \u2014 no state, no I/O, no monkeypatching needed. So no cleanup needed. But the instructions say to make sure to clean up; since there's no st